experiment where every intervention gets a fair shot at changing people's minds. He did it with race, so we can begin with the race IAT, where 70% of white people and even Asians associate white with good and black with bad. you can begin with something like that. And then he asked people, come up with a certain minutes. I forget how long, but your intervention can't be longer than a few minutes. What do you think would work? And it's a lovely way to do science because you're not prejudging what will work and what will not work.
他用种族做了实验，所以我们可以从种族IAT开始，其中70%的白人和甚至亚洲人都将白色与好联系在一起，将黑色与坏联系在一起。

44
00:28:46.000 --> 00:29:26.000
You get to, if you put the work into building that kind of experiment, he'll load it up on the website and get data on it and tell all of us which ones worked and which ones didn't. and a paper was published with many authors to show which ones worked and which ones didn't. So interventions that he tried came in three different categories. Intervention number one, you tell the subject, try to be egalitarian. Do not be biased. Try to treat these two groups equally. The second is you don't say anything to them, but you just give them new associations. So you pair black with good things, white with not so good things, to see
干预措施一：你告诉受试者，尽量保持平等主义。

45
00:29:26.000 --> 00:30:00.000
if that will just shift the IAT bias away from the default in our society and towards whatever you've just taught people. If that works, then that tells us that even implicit biases are open to intervention and to change. So that's number two. I call that sort of the associative measure method or kind of like a brute force method. We're just going to give you new associations to see if they will replace the old ones. And the third intervention is something that I think only humans can do.
我把这称为关联测量方法或某种蛮力方法。

46
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:33.000
You know, we can take any other animal other than us, take non-human animals and give them new associations. And they'll learn those, as we would. But there is something unique to humans, and that's language and the ability to be able to soar in our minds and think entirely new thoughts. And so the third kind of intervention is one where you are asked to imagine, imagine. Imagine a world in which you're suffering in some way, and a person from group A, black, helps you, while a person from group B, white, does not help you.
你知道，除了我们之外，我们可以带任何其他动物，带非人类动物，给它们新的联想。

47
00:30:33.000 --> 00:31:01.000
So you imagine that. Does that matter? And the data show that what works is exactly the opposite order of the way I gave you the three categories. If you use that kind of linguistically based thinking, imagining, somebody helps you that produces the biggest change of the IAT. You actually begin to become more pro-black than you were before, or less empty black than you were before.
想象一下。

48
00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:29.000
The second method, the associations, they work, but not as powerfully. And this was a surprise to us, because I had been thinking that that one would work the best. The IAT's measuring associations were messing directly with the association, so that should produce the biggest effect. It does not. This is why I love science, you know, that every day I can make a hypothesis and what are my students will show me, I'm wrong. And now we know something. We know something new and better.
这就是为什么我热爱科学，你知道，我每天都可以提出一个假设，而我的学生会向我展示，我是错的。

49
00:31:29.000 --> 00:32:31.000
And the worst method, the one that actually has no effect at all, is to tell people to be good people, to be egalitarian and so on. So let me say that this may make it sound like, oh, so it's easy to change. It is easy in the sense that it will last for a short period of time, but it won't last very long. You step out of that experiment and you enter the real world, and the real world is again giving you data that make the same old, same old association between black and bad, white and good, and so your brain snaps back. Now, when young people encounter this result, when they see that, yes, they were able to make change, but the change doesn't last, they get very sad because they want a better world. And I'm not at all sad about that. I think it's telling us the exact truth about how the human mind. mind works. Can you imagine this? We are giving people a tiny little intervention, and it is producing a shift, but the shift does not last. Isn't that a wonderful thing? Because think about all the
而最糟糕的方法，也就是那种完全没有效果的方法，就是告诉人们要做好人、要平等之类的话。所以我想说，这可能会让人觉得，哦，改变起来很简单。从短期来看确实很容易，但不会持续太久。你走出那个实验，进入现实世界，而现实世界再次向你提供数据，让你在黑人与坏、白人与好之间建立起老一套的联想，于是你的大脑又弹回去了。现在，去年轻人遇到这个结果时，当他们看到，是的，他们能够做出改变，但改变却无法持久时，他们会感到非常难过，因为他们想要一个更好的世界。我对此一点也不难过。我认为这告诉了我们关于人类思维如何运作的绝对真理。你能想象吗？我们给人们一个微小的干预，它产生了一种转变，但这种转变并没有持续下去。这不是一件奇妙的事吗？因为想想所有这些

50
00:32:31.000 --> 00:33:17.000
apologize for the word, the crap that we hear all the time, you know, imagine if that were to stick forever, you wouldn't want that kind of a brain. So our brains change, our minds change, associations move around, but they always will gravitate to whatever is your cultural default. And so to bring about actual change, society around us has to change. And then we will move, and then the default will be a new default. So I'm not bothered by this result. I would never predict that the effect should last very long because it would be a dangerous thing if the effects lasted long, because we don't get to choose everything we see in here. So it's a good that we get to snap back to something.
请原谅我用这个词，也就是我们一直听到的那些废话，你知道，想象一下如果那种想法永远留存下来，你肯定不会想要那种大脑。所以我们的大脑在改变，我们的思维在改变，各种联想在四处移动，但它们总是会回归到你的文化默认值。因此，要实现真正的改变，我们周围的社会必须改变。然后我们才会改变，接着默认值就会变成一个新的默认值。所以我并不为这个结果烦恼。我从不预测这种效应能持续很长时间，因为如果效应持续很久的话将会是一件危险的事，因为我们无法选择我们在其中看到的一切。所以，能够弹回到某种状态是一件好事。

51
00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:44.000
So you've been studying implicit bias for decades now, and you have found some evidence that in recent years, some of our biases have decreased. What is happening? How has that happened? How are we measuring it? And what are the biases that are different than they might have been 10 or 20 years ago? It is among the most exciting work that's been happening in my lab. And the person to credit here is a brilliant postdoc, was a graduate student.
所以你研究隐性偏见已经几十年了，你发现了一些证据，表明近年来我们的一些偏见已经减少了。发生了什么？这是怎么发生的？我们是如何测量它的？与10年或20年前相比，现在的偏见有什么不同？这是我实验室里进行的最令人兴奋的工作之一。这里应该归功于一位才华横溢的博士后，他曾是一名研究生。

52
00:33:44.000 --> 00:34:33.000
Tessa Charlesworth. As you know, we opened the website where implicit bias tests were made available in 1998. And here's the story about the beginning of it. There was very little on the internet. There was no psychology experiment happening on the internet. The internet was not very well developed. People then, even large corporations would have kind of one picture with their name and some URL and some phone numbers. That was what was on the internet. And, you know, here we are, we say, let's put these tests on the internet after four, four years of testing it internally in the lab. Once we felt we had a good handle on what it was doing and all the variations that we needed to do to establish basic issues, we put these tests on a
苔丝·查尔斯沃思（Tessa Charlesworth）。如你所知，我们在1998年开通了提供隐性偏见测试的网站。故事的开始是这样的。当时互联网上的内容非常少。互联网上没有任何心理学实验。当时互联网发展得并不好。那时候，即使是大公司也只有一张带有名字、某个网址和一些电话号码的图片。这就是互联网上的内容。你知道，当时我们说，在实验室内部测试了四年之后，让我们把这些测试放到互联网上吧。一旦我们觉得自己对它在做什么有了很好的把握，并且掌握了为了确立基本问题所需的所有各种变化，我们就把这些测试放到了一个

53
00:34:33.000 --> 00:35:26.000
website and we thought, and I remember very explicitly saying to my two colleagues, Tony Greenwald, and Brian Nosek, who actually built the website, I said to them, wouldn't it be amazing? like we don't have to bring one subject in at a time. And maybe in a year we'll have 500 people who will have taken this test and we'll have so much data and they won't even be, you know, Yale and University of Washington students. So people will actually have to take these data seriously because they will come from real people, not from college students. I mean, we were so naive because in the very first month after the site opened, we had 45,000 completed tests. And we were not only taken aback, we had to figure out a coping strategy because people were now writing to us. Dozens of messages in a given day.
网站上，而且我记得我非常明确地对我的两个同事托尼·格林沃尔德（Tony Greenwald）和布莱恩·诺塞克（Brian Nosek）说过，——实际上是布莱恩·诺塞克搭建了这个网站——我对他们说，这难道不神奇吗？就像我们不必一次只带一个受试者进来一样。也许一年内就会有500人参加这个测试，我们将拥有如此多的数据，而且他们甚至不会是（你知道的）耶鲁大学和华盛顿大学的学生。所以人们真的会不得不认真对待这些数据，因为它们来自真实的人，而不是大学生。我的意思是，我们太天真了，因为在网站开通的第一个月，我们就完成了45,000份测试。我们不仅大吃一惊，还不得不找出应对策略，因为人们现在开始给我们写信了。一天之内就有几十条消息。

54
00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:51.000
Your test is stupid. You know, I am not, I do not have a race bias. I am a rap musician. How dare you tell me I'm. So this is what we had to deal with. Anyway, to make a long story short, you know, we think something like maybe we don't count anymore, but there at least 40 million tests have been now taking. and we have an awful lot of data. From about 2007, because we were fiddling with things and we were just getting used to how to build it,
你的测试太蠢了。你知道的，我不是，我没有种族偏见。我是一名说唱音乐家。你怎么敢说我是。所以这就是我们不得不处理的事情。总之，长话短说，你知道，我们觉得可能我们不再被计算在内了，但现在至少已经进行了大约4000万次测试，我们拥有大量的数据。从2007年左右开始，因为我们在调整各种事情，并且刚刚习惯了如何构建它，

55
00:35:51.000 --> 00:36:29.000
we don't believe that the data we got before 2007 were as stable, as reliable, as good as the data from 2007 onwards. So from there onwards, there are lots of data, and the group has put these data on a public website. So anybody can go there. Anybody can take those data and analyze them. and in fact, many people have. So Tessa Charlesworth decided to look at what has happened over time. These are not your typical psychological analyses, right? We do do some longitudinal work.
我们认为2007年之前得到的数据不如2007年及之后的数据那么稳定、可靠和优质。所以从那时起，有大量的数据，团队已经将这些数据放到了一个公开的网站上。所以任何人都可以去那里。任何人都可以获取这些数据并进行分析，事实上，许多人也确实这样做了。因此，苔丝·查尔斯沃思决定研究随着时间的推移发生了什么。这些并不是你典型的心理学分析，对吧？我们确实做一些纵向研究。

56
00:36:29.000 --> 00:37:11.000
Developmental psychologists do study what happens to a child as the child develops and so on. But this data set, I think, is so I generous. There is no such database where we've been. been collecting data continuously 24-7 since 2007 to today. So it's just unusual, you know, a gallop poll, however wonderful it might be, is going to go in and in a single moment get data from 2,500 people or whatever and then conclude that Americans do this or that. We're collecting data every second, every minute, you know, of the day and night because we also opened a bunch of international sites. And so truly data are being collected around.
发展心理学家确实会研究儿童在成长过程中发生的事情等等。但我认为这个数据集是如此慷慨。没有哪个数据库能像我们这样，从2007年到今天，全天候24小时持续收集数据。所以这很不寻常，你知道，盖洛普民意调查无论多么棒，也会在某个单一时刻获取2500人左右的数据，然后得出结论说美国人做了这个或那个。我们一年到头每一秒、每一分钟都在收集数据，因为我们也开通了许多国际网站。所以数据确实在夜以继日地被收集。

57
00:37:11.000 --> 00:38:00.000
around the clock. And we have, for many tests, take race, which is a very popular test, we now have data from dozens and dozens of people in every little location, every county of the United States. So what we can say is, okay, we've got that big, big fat pool of data. Can we look to see if anything has changed in 2007 to today? And again, I should tell you, I was hesitant about even taking this on because I was so persuaded that implicit bias cannot change in the long term that I thought, okay, do this as an exercise for your statistics class, but it's not really something that, you know, we'll be able to say anything about. We will be able to say, I think.
日夜不停。对于许多测试，比如种族测试（这是一个非常受欢迎的测试），我们现在拥有美国每一个小地点、每一个县的成千上万人的数据。所以我们可以说，好吧，我们有了那个庞大而丰富的数据池。我们能不能看看2007年到今天有什么变化？再次说明，我应该告诉你，我甚至对承担这项工作感到犹豫，因为我太确信隐性偏见在长期内是无法改变的，以至于我想，好吧，把这个当作你统计学课的一个练习吧，但它并不是那种（你知道的）我们能够说出点什么的事情。我想我们能够说的是，

58
00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:32.000
Nothing has changed. It's implicit bias in my mind, having looked at the data, having studied it for about 20 years by then, more than that, 30 years maybe. I was quite persuaded that we would see flat lines over time 2007 to when we first did the analysis 2016. No change. And maybe we should write about that. Maybe we should say, look, implicit bias is not changing, et cetera. So Tessa not only did the analysis, she did it not just for one or two groups, but for six of them.
什么都没有改变。在我的脑海中，在看过这些数据、研究了大约20年甚至更长时间（也许30年）之后，我非常确信，从2007年到我们2016年首次进行分析这段时间里，我们会看到平坦的曲线。没有变化。也许我们应该写写这个。也许我们应该说，看吧，隐性偏见并没有改变，等等。所以苔丝不仅做了分析，她不仅为一个或两个群体做了分析，而是为其中的六个群体做了分析。

59
00:38:32.000 --> 00:39:19.000
She looked at anti-gay bias. She looked at race bias. She looked at skin tone bias, which is a little different than race. bias, of course, age bias, disability, and body weight bias. So there are six different kinds of categories. And what she discovered that surprised us is that on three of those tests, you do see substantial change. I'll begin with the one that showed such big change that we're still gobsmacked from having seen those data. And that's anti-gay bias. So anti-gay bias has dropped off by 64% between 2007 and 2020. How can this be? How can a bias that was so high in 2007,
她研究了反同性恋偏见。她研究了种族偏见。她研究了肤色偏见，这当然与种族偏见略有不同，还有年龄偏见、残疾偏见和体重偏见。所以有六种不同的类别。她发现的事情让我们感到惊讶，那就是在这其中的三项测试中，你确实看到了实质性的变化。我先从那项展现出如此巨大变化、以至于我们看到这些数据时至今仍惊愕不已的测试开始。那就是反同性恋偏见。所以，反同性恋偏见在2007年至2020年间下降了64%。这怎么可能？一种在2007年如此高的偏见，

60
00:39:19.000 --> 00:40:08.000
it was one of the large biases, gay, bad, straight good. How come that bias has been just withering away at this speed? And remarkably, if you look at any demographic group in the country, you can look at elderly people, young people, you can look at people who live in the coasts in the middle of the country, the north, the south, the rich, the poor, the educated, the less educated. You can look at any of these. And we see change in every single group. So everybody's changing on this. Yes, there are two groups that are changing faster than everybody. Your listeners should try to predict what those groups might be. And as they do that, I'll give the answer. Younger people are changing faster.
它是最大的偏见之一，即同性恋=坏，异性恋=好。为什么这种偏见会以这样的速度逐渐消退？值得注意的是，如果你看看这个国家的任何一个群体，你可以看看老年人、年轻人，你可以看看住在海岸边的人、住在国家中部的人、北方人、南方人、富人、穷人、受过教育的人、受教育较少的人。你可以看看其中的任何一个。我们在每一个单一的群体中都看到了变化。所以每个人都在这方面发生改变。是的，有两个群体的改变速度比其他人更快。你们的听众可以尝试预测一下这些群体可能是哪些。在他们这样做的同时，我会给出答案。年轻人改变得更快，

61
00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:41.000
and people who are self-professed liberals are also changing faster. In fact, those two groups have pulled away from their original bias in 2007 so fast that they are today at neutrality. Wow. For them, gay and straight are equally good and bad. The rest of us will catch up. So one of the nice things about this analysis, these are called time series analysis. And we use a particular model called Arema for those who are. who might want to go look up what it is.
自称是自由派的人也改变得更快。事实上，这两个群体在2007年迅速脱离了他们最初的偏见，以至于他们今天达到了中立状态。哇。对他们来说，同性恋和异性恋同样好也同样坏。我们其他人会赶上来的。所以这项分析的好处之一是，这些被称为时间序列分析。对于那些可能想去查阅它是什么的人，我们使用了一种称为求和自回归移动平均模型（Arima）的特定模型。

62
00:40:41.000 --> 00:41:06.000
There are lots of limitations to this method. We have to make very sure that we're not just showing this effect because all of a sudden, in recent years, there are many more young people taking the test than they were before. That would not be a correct way to interpret the data to say there is changed. It's not really changed. It's just that the sample has more young people today and didn't have as many young people. So you have to rule out lots and lots of things.
这种方法有很多局限性。我们必须非常确定，我们展示这种效应并不是因为近年来突然有更多的年轻人参加测试，而以前没有。将数据解释为“有变化”并不是一种正确的方式。这其实并不是真正的变化，只是今天的样本中有更多的年轻人，而以前没有那么多年轻人。所以你必须排除许许多多的事情。

63
00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:25.000
So if they're interested, they should go look at this paper. that was published in psychological science. And what they will see if you get into the data is that this change, we think, is real. This is a real shift in what people are thinking. And when I tell people that these days, they say, oh, yes, of course. I could have predicted that that would happen.
因此，如果他们感兴趣，他们应该去看看这篇发表在《心理科学》（Psychological Science）上的论文。如果你深入了解这些数据，你会看到的是，我们认为这种变化是真实的。这是人们思想上的一次真实转变。当我这些天告诉人们这一点时，他们会说，哦，是的，当然。我本可以预测到会发生这种事。

64
00:41:25.000 --> 00:42:07.000
Well, you know, as we know, hindsight is 2020, and they can. But in retrospect, I too now see why this might have happened. So I would love for your listeners while we talk to be thinking about this. Why did sexuality bias change? Because no other bias has changed as fast. Race bias has changed, but by 25%. Okay. So good news is it has moved. But we can ask, why not at the level of 64%. We know that's possible. So why is race slow? Why is skin tone bias going down? Again, by 25%. Why is it not as fast as sexuality? We know that,
好吧，正如我们所知，事后诸葛亮总是百分之百准确，他们确实能预测。但现在回想起来，我也明白了为什么这可能会发生。所以我很乐意让你们的听众在我们的谈话中思考这个问题。为什么性取向偏见会改变？因为没有任何其他偏见改变得这么快。种族偏见确实改变了，但幅度是25%。好吧。好消息是它已经发生了变化。但我们可以问，为什么没有达到64%的水平？我们知道这是可能的。那么为什么种族偏见很慢呢？为什么肤色偏见在下降？同样，下降了25%。为什么它不像性取向那样快？我们知道，

65
00:42:07.000 --> 00:43:03.000
gender bias, home and career bias, has come down by about that much, but why so slow? The association that men are for STEM and science and math and women okay for arts and so on, that bias has, but not as much as the anti-gay bias has. There are three biases that have not shifted much at all, in fact. And that's anti-elderly bias, which continues on and is absolutely stable, a little bit lower, but not anything to write home about. Disability bias, just flat since 2007, no change. And bodyweight bias, a bias against people who are overweight, that remains flat, and that looks like the hardest one in some ways in the data that will change. we think these biases are going to be around.
性别偏见、家庭与职业偏见已经下降了大约那么多，但为什么这么慢？男人适合STEM、科学和数学，而女人适合艺术等等的这种关联偏见已经有所下降，但远没有反同性恋偏见下降得那么多。事实上，有三种偏见几乎没有什么转变。这就是反老年人偏见，它持续存在且绝对稳定，稍微低了一点，但没有什么值得大惊小怪的。残疾偏见，自2007年以来一直持平，没有变化。体重偏见，一种针对超重人群的偏见，仍然保持平稳，在数据中这似乎是某种程度上最难改变的一种。我们认为这些偏见将会一直存在。

66
00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:38.000
If they move at this rate, we're not going to see change for 200 years. That's our prediction. So then what is it that special about anti-gay bias? And I'd like to put a few ideas out and then see if your listeners would agree. I think that sexuality bias changed because it became personal in some way. That is to say, parents and grandparents, friends, neighbors, cousins, we all discover. the people who were close to us, said to us, I'm gay. That became possible.
如果它们以这种速度移动，我们在200年内都不会看到变化。这是我们的预测。那么，反同性恋偏见的特别之处究竟在哪里呢？我想提出几个想法，然后看看你们的听众是否同意。我认为性取向偏见之所以改变，是因为它在某种程度上变得个人化了。也就是说，父母和祖父母、朋友、邻居、表亲，我们都发现，那些与我们亲近的人对我们说，我是同性恋。这成为了可能。

67
00:43:38.000 --> 00:44:11.000
And as they began to come out, we would have confronted a state of dissonance, of cognitive dissonance, this old idea in psychology. I believe being gay is bad. Here's my child saying, she's gay. I have to do something in my head to resolve this conflict. And Leon Fester, the genius in the 1950s who wrote about this theory of cognitive dissidents said, we can't maintain these. We have to change one or the other.
当他们开始出柜时，我们将会面临一种不和谐状态，即认知失调的状态——这是心理学中的一个老概念。我认为同性恋是坏的。这里我的孩子说，她是同性恋。我必须在我的脑海中做点什么来解决这个冲突。莱昂·费斯廷格（Leon Festinger）是20世纪50年代撰写关于这种认知失调理论的天才，他说，我们无法维持这些。我们必须改变其中之一。

68
00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:51.000
Either you have to cut off relations with your child and say, I disown you. Or you can begin to say, I think being gay is not the horrible thing that the Bible told me or whatever, however you came to those beliefs. And what we have found is that maybe it would have gone the other way. I had this happened two centuries ago, but given the time in which we live and the meaning of what religion is to many of us, where we believe that it might be a guide to certain ways of living a good life, but that we may not want to take the Bible. So literally or whatever. It just so happened that people went with love. The love of the child, the love of your neighbor or your friend trumped this other belief.
要么你必须切断与孩子的关系，说，我与你断绝关系。或者你可以开始说，我认为同性恋并不是圣经或其他什么东西告诉我的那种可怕的事情，无论你是如何形成这些信仰的。我们发现的是，也许事情会走向另一个方向。如果两个世纪前发生这种事，但鉴于我们生活的时代以及宗教对我们许多人的意义（我们相信它可能是通往美好生活的某种生活方式的指南，但我们可能不想把圣经看得那么字面化或其他什么），事情恰好是人们选择了爱。对孩子的爱、对邻居或朋友的爱压倒了这种其他的信仰。

69
00:44:51.000 --> 00:45:21.000
And so we've seen change. This does not happen for anti-elderly bias, for race. where we are so segregated. And think about race. You know, the two big domains in which we live and do our stuff are home, the neighborhood, the family, and work. And in both those domains, we're deeply segregated by race. We just don't see people across race lines because it gets intersected with socioeconomic
所以我们看到了改变。这并没有发生在使用反老年人偏见和种族偏见上，在这些领域我们是如此地被隔离。想想种族。你知道，我们生活和做事的两个主要领域是家庭、邻里、家庭和工作。在这两个领域中，我们按种族进行了深刻的隔离。我们只是看不到跨越种族界限的人，因为它与社会经济

70
00:45:21.000 --> 00:46:07.000
class and so on. So the chance that you would meet that person and have the same kind of. of experience that, you know, straight parents and straight grandparents had to deal with when the time came, that just is not an opportunity that race offers us. So as a society, we have to ask ourselves, what will we do if we care about race bias? The second thing that happened for sexuality is that Hollywood got involved. Institutions are deeply important. To Hollywood, this particular dimension matters because there is a larger number of gay people in the entertainment industry than many other industries. I think they cared about it. So they did something remarkable. They made movies
阶层等因素相交织。因此，你遇到那个人并拥有与直人父母和直人祖父母在时机到来时必须处理的同种经历的机会，那根本不是种族提供给我们的机会。因此，作为一个社会，我们必须问自己，如果我们关心种族偏见，我们会做什么？对于性取向发生的第二件事是好莱坞介入了。体制是极其重要的。对好莱坞而言，这个特定的维度很重要，因为娱乐产业中的同性恋人数比许多其他产业要多。我认为他们在乎这一点。所以他们做了一件非凡的事。他们制作了电影

71
00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:43.000
and they made TV shows, not just ordinary ones, but ones in which the gay character would be cooler and smarter and nicer than the straight people on the show. And I don't know, people watched those and came to see that this was being normalized. And we're very sensitive to that. What do other people in our culture think? If I hear that, you know, 90% of my neighbors are saving energy by doing certain things, I become very competitive and I want to be better than them and I will do it. Much more so than if you ask me to be a good person and care about the environment, that doesn't do very much.
他们制作了电视节目，不仅仅是普通的节目，而是其中的同性恋角色比节目中的直人更酷、更聪明、更好的节目。我不知道，人们观看了这些节目，并开始看到这正在变得正常化。我们对此非常敏感。我们文化中的其他人是怎么想的？如果我听到（你知道的）90%的邻居通过做某些事情在节约能源，我就会变得非常好胜，我想比他们做得更好，我也会去做。这远比你要求我做一个好人并关心环境要有效得多，后者并不能起到多大作用。

72
00:46:43.000 --> 00:47:14.000
But hearing that other people do. We're very competitive, yes. They're very competitive, especially socially, of course. So I think that that helped a great deal. And then, of course, the hand of government came in. The Massachusetts Supreme Court first said gay marriages legalized. I remember I had moved to Cambridge only a couple years before this happened. And Boston announced that it would open its courtrooms on whatever it was Monday morning at 9 a.m.
但是听到其他人也在做。我们确实非常好胜，对吧。他们非常好胜，当然，尤其是在社交方面。所以我觉得这在很大程度上起到了帮助作用。然后，当然，政府之手介入了。马萨诸塞州最高法院首先宣布同性婚姻合法化。我记得在这件事发生前几年我刚搬到剑桥。波士顿宣布它将在某个星期一早晨9点开启法庭大门

73
00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:52.000
and performed the first gay marriages. And Cambridge, the suburb, not wanting to be second, decided that it would always open its courtrooms at one minute past midnight to be the first city to do it. So having lived in a town that was the first and having stood there watching the first gay, married couples walking out, it was clear that this was a moment, something historic had just happened. So now think about this. How many times in our history has change come about at three days.
并举行首场同性婚礼。而剑桥作为郊区，不甘落后，决定总是在午夜过一分钟开启法庭大门，成为第一个这样做的城市。所以，既然在一个成为第一的城镇生活过，并且站在那里看着第一对同性已婚夫妇走出来，很显然，这是一个时刻，刚刚发生了某种具有历史意义的事情。所以现在想想这个。在我们的历史上有多少次，改变是在三个不同层面上发生的——

74
00:47:52.000 --> 00:48:44.000
different levels, the individual, the organizational or institutional, and at the level of governmental policies and the law within a short period of time. Yes, the civil rights movement in the 60s did something at that third level, but I don't think that we changed much at the institutional or individual levels. So it could not have the same impact as I think this did. So this is a very important lesson for us, that if we want to make change, maybe one of the messages from our researchers that try and do it at all three levels. And for that reason, I think we should be pleased that people in our society today use the word systemic so much more than we have. Systemic does not mean systematic. Systemic means that the system needs to get engaged. And I would like to argue
不同的层面，即个人层面、组织或体制层面，以及在短时间内政府政策和法律的层面。是的，60年代的民权运动在第三个层面做了一些事情，但我认为我们在体制或个人层面并没有太大改变。所以它不可能产生我认为这所能产生的相同影响。因此，这对我们来说是一个非常重要的课，如果我们想要做出改变，也许我们研究人员的信息之一就是尝试在所有三个层面上进行改变。出于这个原因，我认为我们应该感到高兴的是，今天我们社会中的人们使用“系统性”（systemic）这个词的频率比以前高得多。系统性并不意味着系统化。系统性意味着系统需要参与进来。我想争辩的是

75
00:48:44.000 --> 00:49:08.000
that the system consists of individual people, the system consists of institutions, the system consists of large governmental organizations that can move certain kinds of levers. I cannot say that one is more important than the other, but I would like to argue that if we go for system change, we will see change a lot faster. Well, Dr. Banaji, this has been amazingly interesting.
